    {
      "id": 99,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        1820.0,
        300.0
      ],
      "size": [
        620.0,
        1240.0
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 25,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "title": "Read me",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "# Image to video -- STG + APG\n\nThe heavy-guidance path: per-sigma-band CFG, spatio-temporal guidance and\nadaptive projected guidance on top of image conditioning.\n\n**This is the slow one** -- STG runs a third conditioning pass, so a step costs\n~1.6x a plain CFG step and the whole render is ~3x the distilled graph. Use it\nwhen a scene needs the extra structural coherence, not as a default.\n\nIt composes with image conditioning cleanly: `LTXVImgToVideoConditionOnly` works\non the latent and its noise mask, and never touches the conditioning, so STG's\nperturbed pass sees the same conditioned first frame as the positive pass.\n\n## Combining with SageAttention\n\nSTG and `LTXV Attention Backend` compose -- STG rebinds the attention global and\ndelegates to the binding it captured, which still consults the override.\n\nThey do **not** compose with fused attention nodes that replace `attn1.forward`\noutright (e.g. KJNodes' `LTX2MemoryEfficientSageAttentionPatch`). Those never\ncall `optimized_attention`, so STG silently perturbs the cross-attention instead\nof the self-attention. This pack logs a warning when it detects that.\n\n## How the image gets in\n\n`LTXVImgToVideoConditionOnly` VAE-encodes the image and **writes it over the\nfirst latent frame**, then sets a noise mask of `1 - strength` on those frames.\nIt never touches the conditioning, so it composes with anything -- CFG, STG, APG,\na distilled LoRA -- without the guide-mask attention path.\n\n    LoadImage -> ImageScale (lanczos, centre crop) -> LTXVPreprocess (crf 18)\n              -> LTXVImgToVideoConditionOnly -> LTXVConcatAVLatent -> sampler\n\n**`strength` is the dial that matters.** It is *adherence to the still*, and it\nis inverted into a noise mask, so:\n\n| strength | behaviour |\n|---|---|\n| 1.0 | first frame is locked. Sharpest match, stiffest motion, most likely to sit still for half a second before anything moves. |\n| **0.7** | **the default here.** Holds the composition, lets the model re-time and re-light the opening. |\n| 0.4-0.5 | treats the image as a strong style/composition hint. Use when the still is off-distribution -- an illustration, a screenshot, a render. |\n| 0.0 | equivalent to T2V. |\n\n**`LTXVPreprocess` is not optional in spirit.** LTX was trained on compressed\nvideo; a pristine still is off-distribution, and the model spends its early steps\n\"correcting\" it. That shows up as a texture pop between frame 0 and frame 1.\n`img_compression = 18` is upstream's value.\n\n**Write the prompt about the motion, not the subject.** The subject is already\nfully specified by the image. Prompting the subject again mostly buys you a fight\nbetween the two conditionings.\n\n## The image is resized before it is encoded\n\n`ImageScale` at `crop = center` to exactly the latent's pixel size. That is\ndeliberate: `LTXVImgToVideoConditionOnly` will otherwise do its own bilinear\ncentre crop internally, and lanczos is the better downscale from a camera-sized\nstill. Matching exactly makes the internal branch a no-op, so what the VAE\nencodes is exactly what you see on the `ImageScale` output.\n\nAspect ratio mismatches are **centre-cropped, not letterboxed**. If your subject\nis off-centre, crop it yourself first.\n\n## You get sound\n\n`ltx-2.3-22b-dev` is an audio-video DiT. It generates the audio tokens whether or\nnot you decode them, so these graphs decode the audio half through\n`LTXVAudioVAEDecode` and mux it into the video via `CreateVideo`'s `audio` input.\nIt costs one extra VAE decode and nothing in sampling.\n\n## Why this graph looks like it does\n\nTwo things in the earlier version of these workflows could never have run, and\nboth were found by actually executing them:\n\n**1. The text encoder.** `ltx-2-3-22b-text_encoder.safetensors` is *not* a text\nencoder -- it holds four tensors, `text_embedding_projection.*`. LTX-2.3's text\nencoder is **Gemma-3-12B**, and the projection aggregates its 49 hidden states\n(49 x 3840 = 188160). `comfy/sd.py` only takes the LTX-2.3 Gemma branch\n(`ltxav_te` + `LTXAVGemmaTokenizer`, sd.py:1809) when handed **exactly two**\nstate dicts. A single-file `CLIPLoader(type=ltxv)` falls into the LTX-0.9 **T5**\nbranch instead and dies with `Cannot copy out of meta tensor`.\n\nSo: **`DualCLIPLoader`, type `ltxv`**, Gemma first (sd.py:1812 reads the\ntokenizer from `clip_data[0][\"spiece_model\"]`), projection second. Both live in\n`models/text_encoders`, so nothing is needed in `models/checkpoints`.\n\n**2. The audio branch is not optional.** `ltx-2.3-22b-dev` is an **AV** DiT.\n`av_model.py` builds audio RoPE unconditionally, so a video-only latent gives it\nzero audio tokens and it crashes in `freqs_cis_matrix`. Every upstream 2.3\nworkflow concatenates an audio latent for exactly this reason.\n`LTXVAudioVAELoader` lists from the (empty) `checkpoints` folder, but core\n`VAELoader` reads `models/vae` -- where `LTX23_audio_vae_bf16.safetensors` is.\nThe audio half is split back off and discarded here.\n"
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    }
